189. Rotate Array

Given an integer array nums, rotate the array to the right by k steps, where k is non-negative.

 

Example 1:

Input: nums = [1,2,3,4,5,6,7], k = 3
Output: [5,6,7,1,2,3,4]
Explanation:
rotate 1 steps to the right: [7,1,2,3,4,5,6]
rotate 2 steps to the right: [6,7,1,2,3,4,5]
rotate 3 steps to the right: [5,6,7,1,2,3,4]
Example 2:

Input: nums = [-1,-100,3,99], k = 2
Output: [3,99,-1,-100]
Explanation: 
rotate 1 steps to the right: [99,-1,-100,3]
rotate 2 steps to the right: [3,99,-1,-100]
 

Constraints:

1 <= nums.length <= 105
-231 <= nums[i] <= 231 - 1
0 <= k <= 105
 





Solution:

we are doing a right rotation of the array by k steps 
so we can take the last k elements and 
put them in front of the array and 
then take the first n-k elements and put them after the last k elements.
modulus operator is used to handle the case when k is greater than the length of the array.
and then we will copy the new array to the original array.
incase k is 0 or k is greater than the length of the array we will return the original array as it is.

class Solution {
    public void rotate(int[] nums, int k) {
        if(k>nums.length){
            k=k%nums.length;
        }
        if(k==0 || k>nums.length){
            return;
        }
        int j=nums.length-k;
        int[] arr=new int[nums.length];
        for(int i=0;i<k;i++){
            arr[i]=nums[j];
            j+=1;
        }
        j=0;
        for(int i=k;i<nums.length;i++){
            arr[i]=nums[j];
            j+=1;
        }
        for(int i=0;i<nums.length;i++){
            nums[i]=arr[i];
        }
    }
}